@page "/docs/interfaces"

<PageTitle>Interfaces - FluentCss</PageTitle>

<DocHeader Title="Interfaces"
           Description="Reference for the public FluentCssBuilder entry points and the interfaces that make up the builder and selector API." />

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    This page describes the types you program against. The individual utilities are covered on the
    other pages; here the focus is the shape of the API: the static entry points, what each interface
    is for, and the members it declares.
</p>

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">FluentCssBuilder</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">FluentCssBuilder</code> is the static entry point. Every chain
    starts with one of its three properties, and all three return
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">IRootFluentCssBuilder</code>. The choice only decides the theme
    prefix applied to the whole chain.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@FluentCssBuilderApi" />

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">Builder interfaces</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    The builder stages form a single inheritance chain. A method returns a later (narrower) stage where
    the order matters: <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Pseudo</code> and
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Media</code> return a stage that can still be combined with the
    others, while only <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">IRootFluentCssBuilder</code> can start a media
    query or attach a selector.
</p>

